  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers will continue into Friday across \nthe western Southern Tier.\n\nMid-level low currently over northern Quebec, will allow a shortwave \ndisturbance to round the base today before the trough axis pushes \nsouth across the eastern Great Lakes tonight into Friday. Meanwhile \nat the surface, a cold front will gradually slide south across the \nregion this morning and afternoon, before exiting south tonight. \nOverall, ahead of the front, scattered showers will persist across \nthe western Southern Tier today. Activity will increase this \nafternoon, especially near the New York/Pennsylvania state line as \npeak daytime instability will contribute to shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment.\n\nThen, with the aforementioned trough axis passage Friday, an \nassociated surface trough will slide across the region. With its \npassage Friday morning, a few more scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will pass southeast across western New York.\n\nIn general, instability and shear will remain weak and sub-severe \nkeeping activity to garden variety thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Quieter weather, overall, expected Thursday night \nthrough Saturday.\n\nMid-level ridge and corresponding surface high pressure will ridge \nacross the Great Lakes this weekend, where associated dry air and \nsubsidence will support a drying trend for the weekend. In addition \nto the dry air, temperatures will be 10 degrees cooler than the past \ncouple of days, supporting highs in the low to mid 70s. \n\nClear skies beneath the surface high, the cooler airmass overhead, \nweak surface winds, along with lingering low level moisture will \nsupport some valley steam fog to develop each night across areas \neast of Lake Ontario, the western Southern Tier and into the Finger \nLakes.",
